{
  // ─── The SOLUTION-STYLE positive control for `assertTreeIsTypeChecked` ─
  //
  // A valid tsconfig whose own program compiles nothing: `files` is empty and
  // `references` points at the config that actually holds the tree — here the
  // fixture tree's real `tsconfig.json`. This is the natural thing for an
  // adopter to declare, because in a solution-style repo the file CALLED
  // `tsconfig.json` has exactly this shape, and nothing about the declaration
  // looks wrong. The reference target is not `composite: true`, which a real
  // solution root's would be; TypeScript loads the project here regardless,
  // and the shape being modelled — an own program with zero files — is the
  // same either way.
  //
  // The reference is deliberately the config that DOES contain `src`. That is
  // what pins the claim: TypeScript loads a project from this file (so the
  // "loaded no project" refusal in `forTree` never fires) and does not follow
  // the reference into the program (so the program holds zero of the tree's
  // files). If either of those ever changes, the assertion in
  // `run-structural-fixtures.ts` that declaring this config crashes the types
  // checks with the wrong-program message is what says so.
  "files": [],
  "references": [{ "path": "./tsconfig.json" }]
}
